%S A401625 0,2,0,2,6,8,6,8,0,2,0,2,6,8,6,8,18,20,18,20,24,26,24,26,18,20,18,20,
%T A401625 24,26,24,26,0,2,0,2,6,8,6,8,0,2,0,2,6,8,6,8,18,20,18,20,24,26,24,26,
%U A401625 18,20,18,20,24,26,24,26,54,56,54,56,60,62,60,62,54,56,54,56,60,62,60,62,72,74,72
%N A401625 Image of 0 under repeated application of the map k -> (3k, 3k+2, 3k, 3k+2).
%C A401625 The terms of this sequence can be obtained using the Cantor process on the segment [0,3^n-1]. E.g., for n=2 we have [0,{1},2,{3,4,5},6,{7},8]. The numbers outside of the braces are terms in this sequence.
%C A401625 All terms in this sequence are members of A005823.
%C A401625 The plot of these terms shows a skewed Cantor's dust in two dimensions.
%C A401625 This is a fractal set where the plot of the first 4^k terms looks similar to the first 4^(k+1) terms.
%H A401625 David Cleaver, <a href="/A401625/b401625.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..4095</a>
%H A401625 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantor_set">Cantor set</a>.
%e A401625 0 --> 0 2 0 2 --> 0 2 0 2 6 8 6 8 0 2 0 2 6 8 6 8 --> 0 2 0 2 6 8 6 8 0 2 0 2 6 8 6 8 18 20 18 20 24 26 24 26 18 20 18 20 24 26 24 26 ...
%o A401625 (PARI) a(n)={my(d=digits(n,4),s=#d,i,x=0);for(i=1,s,x+=(d[i]%2)*2*3^(s-i));x}
%Y A401625 Cf. A005823, A356248, A289813, A105220, A000302.
%K A401625 nonn,easy,look,new
%O A401625 0,2
%A A401625 _David Cleaver_, Oct 31 2026
